{% extends 'base.html.j2' %}
{% import 'partials.html.j2' as partials with context %}
{% set title = "Data | " ~ site.name %}
{% set description = "The open dataset behind the readings: every record as CSV and JSON, the fields explained, and how to cite it." %}

{% block main %}<div class="grid">
<div class="col-main">
<span class="kicker">Open dataset</span>
<h1>Data</h1>
<p class="lede">Every measurement this project has made, as it was made, with the corrections in the open. {{ row_count }} records so far, growing weekly through the season.</p>

<h2>What is in it</h2>
<p>One row per measurement. Each sampling visit produces a set of rows sharing an activity id: the E. coli count, total coliforms, water temperature, pH, turbidity, nitrate and orthophosphate, each with its method, unit, the 48 hour antecedent rainfall and its source, the tide stage, and a comment field that carries anything a reader would need to judge the number &mdash; plate counts, calibration notes, detection conditions. Nothing has been tidied out.</p>

<h2>Download</h2>
<p><a class='tx' href='/assets/data/rutherford-reach-wq-2026.csv'>rutherford-reach-wq-2026.csv</a> &middot; <a class='tx' href='/assets/data/rutherford-reach-wq-2026.json'>rutherford-reach-wq-2026.json</a></p>
<p>The JSON carries the same records plus the station and criteria metadata. Both files are regenerated together on every site build, so they always match the pages.</p>

<h2>Reading the fields</h2>
<p>The columns follow common water quality data conventions: <code>activity_start_date</code> and <code>activity_start_time</code> are the real clock at collection; <code>characteristic_name</code> names the measurement; <code>result_value</code> and <code>result_unit</code> carry it; <code>result_detection_condition</code> holds non-numeric outcomes such as "too numerous to count" or "not detected"; <code>result_comment</code> is where the honesty lives &mdash; duplicate plate counts, the plated-volume calibration, and any deviation from the standard method.</p>

<h2>How to cite</h2>
<p>Passaic River Water Watch (2026). Rutherford Reach Water Quality Dataset, station {{ station.id }}, Lower Passaic River. Haolin Li. Released under {{ license }}.</p>

<h2>License</h2>
<p>{{ license }}. Use the readings for anything, with attribution. If you find an error, say so &mdash; the contact address is in the footer, and corrections are made in the open.</p>

<h2>What I would want to know if I were downloading this</h2>
<p>The record is short and young. Weeks 1 and 2 carry a plated-volume correction, documented in their comments. Rainfall is currently attributed to an airport station three miles away, named in every row, until the project's own gauge is mounted. No laboratory split has been run yet, so the field method has no external check so far. The <a class='tx' href='/methods/'>methods page</a> states every one of these limits in full.</p>
</div>
<div class="col-rail">
{{ partials.station_panel() }}
<div class="panel">
  <h4>The record so far</h4>
  <dl class="deflist">
    <dt>Rows</dt><dd>{{ row_count }}</dd>
    <dt>Visits</dt><dd>{{ stats.n }}</dd>
    <dt>First</dt><dd class="tx">{{ weeks[0].date_long }}</dd>
    <dt>Latest</dt><dd class="tx">{{ latest.date_long }}</dd>
    <dt>Updated</dt><dd>{{ updated_long }}</dd>
    <dt>License</dt><dd>{{ license }}</dd>
  </dl>
</div>
{{ partials.bands_panel() }}
</div>
</div>{% endblock %}
